From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 23:45:35 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Drive stylo's relative-selector invalidator so :has()
 invalidates

stylo ships a complete relative-selector invalidator but nothing ever
constructed it: Gecko drives it from C++ `RestyleManager`, and blitz has
no equivalent, so the pass simply never ran. `:has()` still appeared to
work because `set_attribute` stamped a blanket `restyle_subtree()` on
the mutated node's parent, which recascades an anchor that is the
parent, or that is a sibling of the mutated node, and nothing else. So
`#p:has(.t)` held at depth 1 and every deeper anchor silently kept its
stale style. That blanket hint carried the upstream TODO asking for
exactly this.

Attribute and state changes: `resolve_stylist` now runs the
invalidator over the pass's snapshots, before `traverse_dom`. stylo's
`invalidated_self` writes RESTYLE_SELF into the anchor's own
`ElementData` and does not mark anything, but the mutation already
marked the whole root-to-node spine `dirty_descendants`, so the
traversal reaches every ancestor and every sibling of an ancestor with
no further marking. Both the element's live keys and the snapshot's are
looked up: the class that just went away is only in the snapshot, so
looking at the element alone would latch the anchor on and never
invalidate it back. Wiring the state map also fixes `:has(:focus)`,
which was dead at every depth because `set_focus` snapshots the node
without stamping the parent hint.

Tree mutations take no snapshot, so they invalidate at mutation time,
while the node is still linked: insertions after the node is linked,
removals before it is unlinked, including the removal half of a
reparent. inherited_search_path is seeded from the parent because a
freshly created element carries no selector flags and
DomMutationOperation::accept rejects those outright. This runs outside
any traversal, and TElement::set_dirty_descendants marks only the node
itself, so the invalidated callback marks the anchor's ancestors dirty;
otherwise a removal's own mark_ancestors_dirty stopped at the anchor's
set bit and the traversal never reached the anchor's subject (WPT
has-in-parent-position and has-in-ancestor-position).

Both passes share a document-level gate so a page with no :has()
anywhere pays nothing. It treats any shadow root as "maybe", since
iter_origins cannot see shadow cascade data and a false negative would
silently drop invalidation. each_custom_state can no longer be todo!(),
because the collector calls it; blitz has no CustomStateSet, matching
has_custom_state returning false, so it is a no-op.

Dropping the blanket hint is what makes this a win rather than a wash.
On a flat 400-child subtree with one unrelated attribute write per
resolve, 28.3ms -> 22.6ms; 200 children 14.6 -> 11.5; 40 children 3.55
-> 2.61.
